    "turn_text": "Yeah, so I watched the whole thing. And by that, I mean, I missed about 10 minutes of it close to the very end and then caught the very end again. But then I watched it again. But listen, every one of those officers that was there on January 6 has a right to tell their story. Every one of them has a right to be angry. Every one of them has a right to share that. I think as As part of that, it was successful. I would like to point out that also amongst the four of them at the table, they have over four decades of tactical law enforcement experience. And very early on in the questioning, Officer Dunn said that they were woefully unprepared that day. And for two and a half hours afterwards, nobody followed up on that question. And I think that's the deficiency sometimes in legislative hearings. Listen, I sat through the Mueller investigation and impeachments in judiciary. But I also think that that is a really important fact that was missed because we have to make sure we know all of the reasons that led up to this. why we make sure it never happens again, but also have to make sure it never happens again in a way that locks down the Capitol. For the last six months, we've had National Guard troops here in uniform. We've had fences up for about half the time there was razor wire around them. That cannot be what happens moving forward. We have to allow people from all over North Dakota to have access to the government and the Capitol. It's one of the things that makes this city great. And so not only do we have to make sure it never happens again, but we have to do it in a way that makes sure the capital remains open and free for the American people to come to.",
