  {
    "episode_id": 47141,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47141.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "All right. So there's two parts about it. One, I think we can get bipartisan support for it. Two, I think it's important because I don't think anybody thinks Amazon is just the middleman in e-commerce anymore. They're not a bookstore. They're not, I mean, they make their own products. They sell their own products. I mean, they are essentially the only game in town for online retail. They'll tell you differently, but if this affects every brick and mortar store in North Dakota, right? If you're going to survive in the 21st century consumer economy, you're going to be doing business most likely on Amazon. And so this bill goes in and comes in and says, let's have a more realistic approach about what your true relationship is in the e-commerce strain. I mean, whether they're delivering their own packages, all of that, and not afford them blanket 230 protection because they claim the status as only a middleman. And that's just not accurate as to how the economy works. The bigger issue with this, Robin, we've been working on this in my office for six, seven months trying to get it right. It really becomes a state. I mean, products liability is often a state legal function and not a federal function. And if you are a 10th Amendment Republican, that's something you have to talk about. But I think, and where we've come in my office and I'm comfortable with this. I think states rights in e-commerce really are kind of a legal fiction. And you need to understand that they're doing business in all 50 states, and this is actually something the federal government should have a role in.",
