  {
    "episode_id": 47141,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47141.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Right. I mean, internet commerce is, by definition, interstate commerce. I mean, you access the server hosting the website you're shopping on probably isn't in your state. The product that you're buying probably doesn't originate in your state. I don't know, I agree with you. I will say that the Section 230 thing, I have kind of a, I kind of have a weird view because on one hand, it's obviously it's been very good for me. It's allowed me to run a very popular political forum in North Dakota for a long, long time where lots of people have commented on it. I think that if the Section 230 protections didn't exist, I might not have allowed comments and say anything blog would be a very different place today. On the other side of that, I also work for the newspaper industry. And our industry does not have a Section 230 exemption or for any sort of Section 230 protection. Even on our websites, if we publish a letter to the editor, we have liability for that letter to the editor. We don't have the same protections that like Facebook, and yet simultaneously we're competing with organizations like Facebook, like Google, like Twitter, for ad dollars for views, they use our content, they monetize our content a lot of times without compensating us. I think the time for section 230 reform, I had my objections to it kind of from a libertarian perspective. I think the time has come. I think we can do this. I think we can do it in a way that leaves the internet, the free and unfettered place we want it to be while getting rid of some of the abuses. I think it's a good thing. Where are the chances? I mean, what are you hearing from Democrats on this stuff?",
