  {
    "episode_id": 47141,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47141.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, you know, we had some bipartisan antitrust bills that came out of the antitrust subcommittee, which is my former committee. Last Congress and I there's two of them that I am on and it's a venue reform bill, but I think. I think there's going to be a real effort from smart people on both sides that have worked together on these issues to find the places where we don't have those partisan fights, because we're always going to have them, right? I mean, 230 as far as liability in that space is going to be a fight, and we're going to have to figure out how to best approach it. I do want to comment on a couple of things you said, because I've been tasked to lead the Republican task force on anti-competitive markets in big tech, and I think the newspaper thing is really important. And for two reasons, one, the vast majority of people who read the Fargo form probably read it on Facebook or Google. Not a lot of people sitting around having a cup of coffee, opening a regular newspaper anymore. You don't get paid for it. They utilize your content. We have to figure out ways in which to move those things forward. But I also, I took a lot of heat for both sides, actually, when I wasn't really necessarily supportive of 230 repeal. Because I think repeal without reform does one of two things. It either walks down the internet or it turns it into an absolute cesspool. Think of the help reviews from when somebody gets mad at a restaurant at some point in time and how that can run. So I think it's important to reform it. But I also just want to point out, and I",
